1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,760
You've probably seen it in the news:

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:04,319
some big AI model has over

3
00:00:04,319 --> 00:00:06,059
a hundred billion parameters.

4
00:00:06,409 --> 00:00:08,287
So what actually is a parameter?

5
00:00:08,287 --> 00:00:10,635
And what does it mean to have that many?

6
00:00:10,985 --> 00:00:13,327
Last time, we saw that today's AI mostly

7
00:00:13,327 --> 00:00:16,079
figures out patterns on its own, from examples.

8
00:00:16,079 --> 00:00:18,363
What it ends up with is called a model.

9
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:19,530
So this time

10
00:00:19,530 --> 00:00:21,163
let's crack a model open

11
00:00:21,163 --> 00:00:23,136
and see what's really inside.

12
00:00:31,467 --> 00:00:33,601
A model is basically a machine:

13
00:00:33,601 --> 00:00:35,528
you feed an input in one end

14
00:00:35,528 --> 00:00:37,869
and an answer comes out the other.

15
00:00:38,219 --> 00:00:39,360
And on this machine

16
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:40,742
there's a row of knobs.

17
00:00:40,742 --> 00:00:42,664
Turn them to different positions

18
00:00:42,664 --> 00:00:44,106
and the same input gives

19
00:00:44,106 --> 00:00:45,247
a different answer.

20
00:00:45,597 --> 00:00:47,311
Those knobs are the parameters.

21
00:00:47,661 --> 00:00:48,778
Here's an example:

22
00:00:48,778 --> 00:00:50,082
guessing a taxi fare.

23
00:00:50,082 --> 00:00:52,316
We put in the distance in kilometers

24
00:00:52,316 --> 00:00:54,489
and out comes a guess at the price.

25
00:00:54,839 --> 00:00:55,769
At its simplest

26
00:00:55,769 --> 00:00:57,878
this machine needs just two knobs:

27
00:00:57,878 --> 00:00:59,180
one for the base fare

28
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:01,413
and one for the price per kilometer.

29
00:01:01,763 --> 00:01:02,657
To make a guess

30
00:01:02,657 --> 00:01:04,506
it multiplies the per-kilometer

31
00:01:04,506 --> 00:01:05,758
price by the distance

32
00:01:05,758 --> 00:01:07,189
then adds the base fare.

33
00:01:07,539 --> 00:01:09,852
Say the base fare is set to $10

34
00:01:09,852 --> 00:01:12,314
and the per-kilometer knob to $2.

35
00:01:12,314 --> 00:01:15,224
For a 5-kilometer ride, it guesses $20.

36
00:01:15,574 --> 00:01:16,646
People decide how

37
00:01:16,646 --> 00:01:18,286
the machine does its math.

38
00:01:18,286 --> 00:01:19,988
But where each knob is set?

39
00:01:19,988 --> 00:01:21,313
Nobody types that in.

40
00:01:21,313 --> 00:01:23,583
The machine learns it from examples.

41
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:25,476
That's all "learning" is:

42
00:01:25,476 --> 00:01:28,006
turning the knobs to the right positions.

43
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:34,401
Here are twelve made-up taxi rides.

44
00:01:34,751 --> 00:01:36,178
Each dot is one ride:

45
00:01:36,178 --> 00:01:38,760
the further right, the longer the trip

46
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:41,206
and the higher up, the more it cost.

47
00:01:41,556 --> 00:01:43,000
Put its guesses on a graph

48
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,499
and the straight line gives

49
00:01:44,499 --> 00:01:46,054
you a fare for any distance.

50
00:01:46,404 --> 00:01:48,296
The little vertical line from each

51
00:01:48,296 --> 00:01:49,965
dot to that line shows how far

52
00:01:49,965 --> 00:01:51,745
off the guess was for that ride.

53
00:01:52,095 --> 00:01:53,661
Let's start with the knobs

54
00:01:53,661 --> 00:01:55,226
set pretty much at random:

55
00:01:55,226 --> 00:01:57,093
$0 base fare, $1 per kilometer.

56
00:01:57,093 --> 00:01:58,418
The line's way too low

57
00:01:58,418 --> 00:02:00,525
and every ride gets underestimated.

58
00:02:00,875 --> 00:02:02,586
Turn the base fare up to $10

59
00:02:02,586 --> 00:02:04,298
and the whole line lifts up.

60
00:02:04,648 --> 00:02:06,650
Now the short rides are pretty close

61
00:02:06,650 --> 00:02:07,929
but the longer the ride

62
00:02:07,929 --> 00:02:09,264
the further off it gets.

63
00:02:09,614 --> 00:02:11,716
So turn the per-kilometer knob

64
00:02:11,716 --> 00:02:13,537
and the line gets steeper.

65
00:02:13,537 --> 00:02:16,409
At $3.50 a kilometer, we've gone too far:

66
00:02:16,409 --> 00:02:19,001
now the long rides are overestimated.

67
00:02:19,351 --> 00:02:20,914
Back the per-kilometer

68
00:02:20,914 --> 00:02:22,548
knob off to about $2.50

69
00:02:22,548 --> 00:02:25,177
and nudge the base fare up to $10.20.

70
00:02:25,527 --> 00:02:27,608
Now the line runs right along the dots

71
00:02:27,608 --> 00:02:28,704
and all those little

72
00:02:28,704 --> 00:02:30,073
vertical lines are short.

73
00:02:30,423 --> 00:02:33,035
Once it's tuned, you can actually use it.

74
00:02:33,035 --> 00:02:33,672
So ask it:

75
00:02:33,672 --> 00:02:36,157
what would a ride of 8 kilometers cost?

76
00:02:36,507 --> 00:02:38,072
None of our twelve rides

77
00:02:38,072 --> 00:02:39,376
was eight kilometers

78
00:02:39,376 --> 00:02:42,244
but it can still make a guess: about $30.50.

79
00:02:46,367 --> 00:02:49,184
So what did this machine actually learn?

80
00:02:49,534 --> 00:02:50,620
Everything it learned

81
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:52,379
from those twelve rides comes down

82
00:02:52,379 --> 00:02:54,086
to where those two knobs are set.

83
00:02:54,436 --> 00:02:56,985
The machine itself hasn't changed one bit.

84
00:02:56,985 --> 00:02:58,685
Only the knob settings have.

85
00:02:59,035 --> 00:03:00,300
Copy those two numbers

86
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:01,507
onto a scrap of paper

87
00:03:01,507 --> 00:03:02,600
hand it to a friend

88
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:04,727
and they've got the exact same model.

89
00:03:05,077 --> 00:03:07,349
When people talk about "training a model,"

90
00:03:07,349 --> 00:03:09,622
all they're really doing is turning knobs.

91
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:14,872
Two knobs are plenty

92
00:03:14,872 --> 00:03:16,297
for guessing a taxi fare.

93
00:03:16,297 --> 00:03:18,120
But if you want a model to write

94
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:19,601
essays or chat with people

95
00:03:19,601 --> 00:03:20,911
you need way, way more.

96
00:03:21,261 --> 00:03:24,432
In 2020, OpenAI released GPT-3

97
00:03:24,432 --> 00:03:27,921
which has 175 billion parameters.

98
00:03:28,271 --> 00:03:29,109
That's a hundred

99
00:03:29,109 --> 00:03:30,732
and seventy-five billion knobs.

100
00:03:31,082 --> 00:03:32,058
How many is that?

101
00:03:32,058 --> 00:03:34,240
Read out one knob setting every second

102
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:35,504
no eating, no sleeping

103
00:03:35,504 --> 00:03:37,054
and it'd take you over five

104
00:03:37,054 --> 00:03:38,547
and a half thousand years.

105
00:03:38,897 --> 00:03:41,741
Obviously, nobody turned all those knobs by hand.

106
00:03:41,741 --> 00:03:43,656
Their settings came from training

107
00:03:43,656 --> 00:03:45,049
on huge amounts of text.

108
00:03:45,399 --> 00:03:47,797
But it's the same idea as the taxi fare:

109
00:03:47,797 --> 00:03:48,996
people pick the math

110
00:03:48,996 --> 00:03:50,734
and the settings are learned.

111
00:03:51,084 --> 00:03:53,499
And a trained model, saved to a file

112
00:03:53,499 --> 00:03:55,109
is mostly just this long

113
00:03:55,109 --> 00:03:56,920
long list of knob settings.

114
00:03:57,270 --> 00:03:58,848
So when the news says "over

115
00:03:58,848 --> 00:04:00,600
a hundred billion parameters,"

116
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:02,003
it means the machine has

117
00:04:02,003 --> 00:04:03,697
over a hundred billion knobs.

118
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:07,981
In short, a model is a machine with knobs.

119
00:04:08,331 --> 00:04:10,006
And learning just means turning

120
00:04:10,006 --> 00:04:11,789
the knobs to the right positions.

121
00:04:12,139 --> 00:04:13,408
But what does "the right

122
00:04:13,408 --> 00:04:14,730
positions" actually mean?

123
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:17,313
Remember those little vertical lines

124
00:04:17,313 --> 00:04:18,678
some long, some short?

125
00:04:18,678 --> 00:04:19,732
They're the clue.

126
00:04:20,082 --> 00:04:21,009
That's next time

127
00:04:21,009 --> 00:04:22,980
when we talk about loss functions.
